- A merchant three‑masted ship, the Pharaon, returns to Marseilles; its young first mate (Armando Pascal here) tells the owner of Captain Leclere’s death and that he followed the captain’s dying orders (an errand that included a stop at Elba). Pascal is well liked by the crew; the ship’s supercargo (Blaire) is hostile. Pascal visits his father and his betrothed (Abrielle — the Catalan girl), and a wedding is arranged. That celebration is interrupted when Pascal is arrested on an anonymous denunciation alleging he carried compromising correspondence from Elba. He’s taken to the Chateau d’If and imprisoned. In the next episodes Pascal meets the cellmate the Trenton Davion (the learned Abbé Faria figure), who becomes his tutor, reveals the existence of a buried treasure (the Spada/Monte Cristo hoard on Brayan Annabel / Monte Cristo), and then dies after a final paroxysm; Pascal later escapes the prison and is rescued by a small Genoese tartan, beginning the path that will lead him toward the island and the treasure.
- Tone: melodramatic adventure, with political backdrop (Napoleonic return), interpersonal betrayals (Blaire/Danglars, Quinton/Fernand, the official who abuses power).

